event: add DownloadProgress for chunked message downloads

Reports permille progress (1..=999, 1000 = done) while a large message
is being downloaded from the server in chunks. Modeled on ImexProgress.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019DhvV6wm3aXyrgHHXaefxw

imap: download large messages in resumable chunks via partial FETCH

Messages fetched on demand (download-on-demand path) are now downloaded
with a sequence of BODY.PEEK[]<offset.count> partial FETCHes (mandatory
IMAP4rev1, RFC 3501) instead of a single full-body FETCH:

- Chunks are appended to a staging file in the blobdir whose length is
  the resume offset; an interrupted download continues where it stopped,
  across reconnects and restarts. Chunk size doubles from 128 KiB to a
  4 MiB cap to amortize round-trip latency. On native platforms peak
  memory drops from O(message size) to O(chunk size); on wasm the
  staging file lives in the in-memory fs, so the win there is
  resumability across short-lived browser tabs, not memory.
- The download yields back to the IMAP loop every 15 seconds so that
  newly arriving messages are fetched in between; the interrupt-driven
  loop returns to the download which resumes from its staging file.
  Time-based rather than chunk-count-based so the new-mail latency
  bound holds on slow links too.
- End of message is detected by an EMPTY out-of-range chunk, never by a
  merely short chunk (a server or proxy delivering fewer bytes than
  requested mid-message must not silently truncate the download), and
  never via RFC822.SIZE, which some servers misreport; the size only
  feeds the new DownloadProgress events.
- The staging name is keyed by (transport, folder hash, UIDVALIDITY,
  UID); on UIDVALIDITY change stale staging files are discarded, and
  housekeeping keeps staging files of pending downloads alive.
- The assembled message must carry the expected Message-ID, guarding
  against servers that reuse UIDs without bumping UIDVALIDITY; a
  mismatch sets DownloadState::Failure instead of retrying forever.
- Fallback for servers without working partial FETCH: async-imap
  terminates a FETCH stream at the tagged response without surfacing
  its NO/BAD status, so a rejection looks like an empty response — the
  empty case is disambiguated by re-probing the UID (message still
  there = partial FETCH refused). Chunked downloads are then disabled
  for the transport for the process lifetime, a one-time device message
  informs the user, and the previous full-body fetch is used instead.
  Responses with a wrong or missing range origin past the start of the
  message are treated the same way rather than guessed at; a server
  that ignores the range but returns the full message from the
  beginning is accepted silently.
- fetch_msgs_mutex is now held only around receive_imf instead of the
  whole (potentially long) download; a message delivered concurrently
  via another transport is detected as duplicate by receive_imf.

Includes tests for the chunk loop (adaptive sizing, resume, size lies,
origin validation, truncation guard, yield-and-resume, error paths
preserving resume state), the staging file lifecycle, UIDVALIDITY-change
discarding, housekeeping GC and the one-time fallback device message.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019DhvV6wm3aXyrgHHXaefxw

download: let a running download be stopped

abort_download(msg_id) dequeues an in-progress download and puts the
message back to Available, so the download button returns. It is one
conditional UPDATE (InProgress -> Available): a download finishing in
between sets Done, which must not be turned back.

A queued download's chunk loop checks before every chunk that its
`download` row still exists and yields if it is gone. It checks the row,
not the download state: queued auto-downloads stay Available with their
row present the whole time. Yielding keeps the staging file, so
downloading again resumes at the byte offset until housekeeping reclaims
the file, and never deletes a row the user re-created by tapping again.

A whole-message fetch on a server without partial FETCH cannot be
interrupted and still completes. Exposed over JSON-RPC as abort_download.
